ned evaporated milk in the  philippines fresh milk isn't as  available it's a hot country so  using evaporated milk is much better  since it's shelf stable and you don't  have to worry about spoiled milk and  next we're going to add sweetened  condensed milk and you're only using  half a cup of this condensed milk and  you want to make sure you use a really  good spatula to kind of get all those  little bits out there's a word in  filipino language called scion basically  it's like oh what a waste what a pity  and you don't want to waste it if  there's one thing that we do at our  store we're really good about making  sure that we have every little bit used  so we're done with the custard but what  sets ours apart is that we add ube to it  if you don't know what ube is it is a  party in your mouth ube is a purple yam  from the philippines specifically  indigenous to the philippines the ube  flavor profile is very similar to almost  vanilla and coconut we can debate ube  all day i mean people say ube's tarot no  it's not ube is a purple potato no it's  not everyone thinks ube tastes different  what do you guys think comment below you  can source this from your local filipino  grocery stores but if you don't have one  near you you can always go online and  purchase it there so i'm going to add  one tablespoon of ube flavoring into  this beautiful custard  look at that  purple goodness vibrant royal purple  color we're not using powders or actual  ube root because it's going to ruin the  texture and we want to be able to stay  true to that texture of flan as being  creamy and silky and really good mouth  feel now that we've finished whisking  this beautiful purple custard we're  going to strain it it's actually one of  the biggest complaints about custard  people don't like eggy custard or has  like texture of eggs as you can see here  all these little eggy bits are left  behind and they're no longer going to  interfere with your beautiful silky  custard and we're going to put the ube  custard first you don't want purple mess  all over just inside of that pie pan  we are going to scrape every little bit  don't want cyan  you want to use every little bit and  you're probably wondering why our pie  dish is in the roasting pan and the  reason why is because we're going to  slow and low cook this we're going to  add a water bath at the bottom basically  it's going to make the custard cook  evenly we're going to fill up the hot  water halfway up the pan as best as you  can estimate you definitely don't want  it close to the lip of the pie pan  because it'll spill inside once you're  transporting this into the oven we  already have a start with the hot water  going into the roasting pan i like to  add  a cover on top of the pan to create  steam so again we want consistent  custard because like a true lucha flan  you want to make sure that it's  consistent throughout and if this didn't  have the foil on top it'll make a little  crust and that's not very good when it  comes to the final product we want to  make a crust not this kind of crust so  i'm going to put this in the oven for 45  minutes at 350 degrees and then after 45  minutes i'm going to remove the foil and  turn it around and bake it for another  10 to 15 minutes and here is the little  purple beauty you just took this out of  the oven so how do you know that it's  completely set if you do a little jiggle  you'll see that it has a slight jiggle  so we're gonna have a chill overnight in  the refrigerator and i'll see in the  morning  good morning  i am wearing the same thing and that's  okay please forgive it we are ready for  the next part which is our ube  upside-down pie graham cracker crust the  reason why we call it ube ups and down  pie is because we have the crust on top  and the crust came about because i made  a mistake and i wanted to save it the  whole thing of scion so we're going to  measure out three and a half ounces of  graham cracker crumbs so now we're gonna  add brown sugar the brown sugar provides  a little molasses type of caramel leaf  sugar next i'm gonna add some greek  yogurt whole milk yogurt again you don't  want to waste some scraping every little  bit out i grabbed some graham cracker  crumbs and i had greek yogurt and brown  sugar so i just mixed it all together  and i'm like this tastes fine this  tastes good and it's sold out it sold  out that following weekend and i'm like  oh my gosh we have a new product don't  be afraid of your mistakes your mistakes  can always be made to something else to  be honest to make a really consistent  crumb i like using my hand so i'm gonna  get in there and kind of crumble it it's  like playing in sand except delicious  delicious sand if you crush it in your  hands it kind of has a little shape but  then you can easily break it down that's  the right amount of moisture so now  we're going to add our crusty crust onto  the ube custard and then push it all the  way to the side so you cover the crust  or you can be a little delicate i'm  going to be a little fancy into a little  circle after you smooth out the crust  we're going to add some granulated sugar  very light sprinkling not super heavy  just enough so that it lightly dusts the  top of the crust after we sprinkle the  sugar we're going to lay the surface of  the crust and not everyone has a torch  at home and that's completely fine we're  going to stick this into the broiler for  one to two minutes to the broiler  we are done so we have this nice smoky  finish on top i'm going to add a little  purple sugar for a little juice the  reason why we put this on is because we  have other upside-down pies so this is  the indicator for us that is our number  one dessert at our store ube upside  amsterdam pie purple yam custard topped  with the brulee graham cracker greek  yogurt crust i'm ready to sample this i  like to put it in a container that you  can see the purple goodness  i'm planning myself on the back for  making th